raft.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   26087 Nov 11 14:27 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   41206 Nov 11 14:31 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 14:08 results -> ../investigation\ndrwx------  2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:46 revisions\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:46 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:25 scripts\ndrwxr-xr-x  7 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:14 source_evidence\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 14:12 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 14:12 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 14:08 task -> ../task\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    7687 Nov 11 14:46 validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    6632 Nov 11 14:46 validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   21078 Nov 11 14:46 validation_report.json\n# Paper validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-11-11T14:46:42.564190+00:00\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- agentic evidence audit failed: see work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json and work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\n- figure validation failed: figures[1] figure_type does not match strategy for slope_interval_vs_decision_rule; figures[2] figure_type does not match strategy for search_variants_descriptive\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `5766`\n- substantive_element_count: `10`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `73`\n- resolved_references: `48`\n- anchored_references: `25`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `6`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- summary: The main-text numbers, the qualification disclosure and the derived resolution quantities match the evaluator metrics and derived/resolution.json. The development-plane supplement has one false comparison and one unsupported causal inference, and a few implementation and process statements cannot be inspected in the artifacts.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `si-dev-span-false` (Supplementary Information, Development-plane comparison (and the claims_grounding.json development claim)): The V3 per-seed range [0.801, 0.899] contains the V2 mean (0.828) but not the V1 mean (0.798), because 0.801 is above 0.798. The claim that it spans both other means is false, and the unqualified claim that no ordering is supported rests on it.\n  - claim: per-seed scores of V3 (0.801, 0.899) spanning the other two means, so no ordering of the configurations is supported\n  - evidence: source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json (score 0.7977); derived/resolution.json search.variants[2].seed_scores (101: 0.8012, 102: 0.8991); figures/search_variants_descriptive.png panel (a), where the V3 seed-101 dot sits above the V1 mean bar; claims_grounding.json claim on development-plane configurations.\n  - requested_fix: Replace the sentence with a supportable one, for example: V3 per-seed scores (0.801, 0.899) span the V2 mean (0.828) and lie just above the V1 mean (0.798). With two seeds per configuration, seed-to-seed spread (0.051, 0.043, 0.098) is as large as or larger than the differences between configuration means (0.030, 0.022). Correct the matching claims_grounding.json text.\n- `censoring-threshold-inference` (Supplementary Information (Development-plane comparison) and Discussion (Threshold and time-grid sensitivity)): The evidence gives only a six-run mean of final held-out accuracy. It does not say which threshold (training or held-out) was missed, in which runs, or whether any run ended near 0.99. Censoring is defined as never reaching 0.99 at any checkpoint, not as ending below it. The paper itself states that censoring merges not-fitting and not-generalizing and that the artifacts cannot separate them. The causal attribution to the threshold is therefore unsupported.\n  - claim: All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is 0.944, illustrating that the 0.99 threshold, not failure to learn, can produce censoring; runs that end below 0.99 held-out accuracy are censored however close they come to it\n  - evidence: source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json (final_val_acc_mean 0.944, censored_fraction 1.0, no per-run fit or generalization steps); paper.tex Censoring paragraph and Scope section; source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py delay_gap (censored if fit or gen is None).\n  - requested_fix: Qualify the sentence. Say only that all six V2 runs were censored while their mean final held-out accuracy was 0.944, and that the retained records do not show whether censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to 0.99. Remove the claim that the threshold, not failure to learn, produced the censoring.\n- `uninspectable-process-claims` (Discussion (What would resolve it, item iv), Supplementary Information (Analyses not performed, Reproducibility)): No sweep script appears in source_evidence/, scripts/ or any other readable artifact. The only support is the agent's internal strategy notes. Nothing in the evaluator records or manifests shows how many times the sealed evaluation was run. The paper cites no inspectable source for either statement.\n  - claim: a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed; the sealed evaluation was run once\n  - evidence: Directory listings of source_evidence/ and scripts/ (only derive_resolution.py, build_figures.py, write_prov.py, and the evaluator and model sources); source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json (a single started_at and completed_at pair, no run-count field); the only mention of the sweep script is in paper_strategy.md and memory.md.\n  - requested_fix: Remove, or qualify as not verifiable from the retained artifacts, the statements that a sweep script exists and that the sealed evaluation was run once. Keep the point that no exponent sweep or matched ablation has results.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[0] labels too long without abbreviation\n- figures[0].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\n- figures[1] labels too long without abbreviation\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\n- figures[2] exceeds series budget in panel 0: 3 > 2\n- figures[2].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1618 < 2400\n# Draft reference gap memo\n\n**Status:** ready. A targeted post-draft retrieval pass is justified for three paragraphs. The rest of the draft is either well cited or grounded in `paper_data.json`.\n\n## What is already adequate\n- The grokking explanations in the introduction and the first Related Work paragraph (Power, Nanda, Liu, Varma, Kumar, Huang, Davies) are cited directly.\n- All quantitative results (slope, interval, censored fraction, resolution limits) are grounded in the evaluator artifacts and need no citations.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Related work, \"Parametrization and optimizer scale\" (high, background)\nThe evaluated model's key design element is a width-dependent multiplier meant to equalize the effective step size under Adam. The Discussion argues this could mask or create a width slope. The paragraph cites only an RL normalization paper and a dynamical-richness measurement paper. The standard width-aware parametrization literature is missing.\n- Search topics: muP / Tensor Programs; hyperparameter transfer across width; Adam parametrization and effective step size.\n\n### 2. Related work and introduction, prior work on width dependence (high, positioning)\nThe paper asks how grokking delay depends on model width. No cited work is positioned as having examined delay against width, size, weight decay, or initialization scale. The \"Relation to this work\" paragraph and the capacity-based reading both need this positioning. The memorization and double-descent citations are only indirect support.\n- Search topics: grokking on modular arithmetic versus width and weight decay; grokking time versus model or data size; initialization scale and effective learning rate in grokking.\n\n### 3. Method, test statistic (medium, background)\nThe statistic is a seed-cluster percentile bootstrap on delays right-censored at the cap, entered at the cap value. The Discussion recommends censored-data analysis. No methodological reference supports either choice or the remedy.\n- Search topics: cluster bootstrap with few clusters; Tobit / accelerated-failure-time regression; bias from imputing censored values.\n\n## Not requested\nStronger citations for memorization, double descent, or the individual grokking explanations. These are adequately covered and would only enlarge the bibliography.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_140816.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_140816.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
