an final held-out accuracy was 0.944, and that the retained records do not show whether censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to 0.99. Remove the claim that the threshold, not failure to learn, produced the censoring.\n- `uninspectable-process-claims` (Discussion (What would resolve it, item iv), Supplementary Information (Analyses not performed, Reproducibility)): No sweep script appears in source_evidence/, scripts/ or any other readable artifact. The only support is the agent's internal strategy notes. Nothing in the evaluator records or manifests shows how many times the sealed evaluation was run. The paper cites no inspectable source for either statement.\n  - claim: a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed; the sealed evaluation was run once\n  - evidence: Directory listings of source_evidence/ and scripts/ (only derive_resolution.py, build_figures.py, write_prov.py, and the evaluator and model sources); source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json (a single started_at and completed_at pair, no run-count field); the only mention of the sweep script is in paper_strategy.md and memory.md.\n  - requested_fix: Remove, or qualify as not verifiable from the retained artifacts, the statements that a sweep script exists and that the sealed evaluation was run once. Keep the point that no exponent sweep or matched ablation has results.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwKfwE21NxiEHTGBA5kg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ehw4e9foopSSXJ6Y6QsQfA","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; grep -n \"sweep script\\|run once\\|exactly once\\|spans\\|span both\\|not failure to learn\\|threshold, not\\|0.944\\|once\" paper.tex | head -40; grep -n \"sweep script\\|once\\|span\\|0.944\" claims_grounding.json | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8583,"cache_read_input_tokens":36679,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8583},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63ce2fa1-bd22-4da1-8787-97637473790b","uuid":"b7be26da-5e4c-4864-969c-76d75ab08c22","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:46:51.594Z","request_id":"req_011CgwKfvUP4mR3BqEQyA2av"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ehw4e9foopSSXJ6Y6QsQfA","type":"tool_result","content":"49:The outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n195:Forty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n248:(a) Little or no width dependence & Similar delays across widths, within seed noise & Censored runs not concentrated at either end \\\\\n249:(b) Negative dependence masked by cap and noise & Longer delays at narrow widths, partly hidden at the cap & Censored runs concentrated at narrow widths \\\\\n256:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), i.e.\\ runs that end below $0.99$ held-out accuracy are censored however close they come to it. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n258:\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap (survival-analysis regression, e.g.\\ \\citealp{Cox1972RegressionMA}, handles right-censored outcomes directly, although we did not apply it), or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n280:\\paragraph{Limitations.} Table~\\ref{tab:claims} summarizes which claims the evidence does and does not support. The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n350:Figure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$, illustrating that the $0.99$ threshold, not failure to learn, can produce censoring. With two seeds per configuration and per-seed scores of V3 ($0.801$, $0.899$) spanning the other two means, no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n370:V2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\n382:The accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The sealed evaluation was run once; the aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n141:   \"claim\": \"Development-plane configurations (modulus 53, fraction 0.5, widths 32/64/128, 2 seeds) scored 0.798, 0.828, 0.850 with 4/6, 6/6, 4/6 runs censored; mean final held-out accuracy 0.981, 0.944, 0.921; per-seed scores of the third span the other two means.\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63ce2fa1-bd22-4da1-8787-97637473790b","uuid":"31895dae-0840-457b-98a8-26e3bdd56dd0","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:46:51.718Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"49:The outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy t