{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017mBojvAzUtJCSf7EhHH74Z","type":"tool_result","content":"49:The outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n195:Forty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n248:(a) Little or no width dependence & Similar delays across widths, within seed noise & Censored runs not concentrated at either end \\\\\n249:(b) Negative dependence masked by cap and noise & Longer delays at narrow widths, partly hidden at the cap & Censored runs concentrated at narrow widths \\\\\n258:\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap (survival-analysis regression, e.g.\\ \\citealp{Cox1972RegressionMA}, handles right-censored outcomes directly, although we did not apply it), or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (no result for such a sweep exists in the retained artifacts); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n280:\\paragraph{Limitations.} Table~\\ref{tab:claims} summarizes which claims the evidence does and does not support. The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n284:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use enough seeds that the cluster bootstrap has a rich resampling support, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.\n378:The following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent (a script exists but was not run); a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n9:      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n38:      \"figure_type\": \"interval_plot\",\n72:      \"figure_type\": \"dot_plot\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63ce2fa1-bd22-4da1-8787-97637473790b","uuid":"3d5bcd43-3a95-45ef-979e-3a4ae7f747a9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:46:58.724Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"49:The outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n195:Forty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n248:(a) Littl